@ENDNODE

@NODE INSTALL "Installing"
@{b}Installing@{ub}

Needs AmigaOS 4.1 (icon.library 51.15 or newer, z.library 53 or newer).

Double-click @{i}Install@{ui}. It copies

  emblem.iconmodule   to LIBS:IconModules
  EmblemIcon          to C:
  the sample images   to ENVARC:sys/emblem
  the settings        to ENVARC:sys/emblem.prefs (and ENV:sys)
  this guide and the  to a drawer "Emblem" in SYS:Documentation
  examples               (or where you choose)

Then @{b}restart the computer@{ub}: icon.library loads icon modules only when
it starts.

Running Install again updates an installed copy; it can also remove
Emblem (see @{"Removing Emblem" link REMOVE}).

By hand: copy the files to the places above and restart.
@ENDNODE

@NODE QUICK "Quick start"
@{b}Quick start@{ub}

Normal icons are never shown to icon modules (icon.library reads them
itself), so an icon must be in the Emblem format first. To give the drawer
Work:Music a music emblem:

1. In a Shell:

     EmblemIcon Work:Music TO=EMBLEM

   The icon looks the same: it still has its own picture.

2. Select the icon, choose @{i}Icons > Information...@{ui} and add these
   tool types:

     IMAGE=DrawerNS
     IMAGESELECTED=DrawerS
     EMBLEM=EmblemMusic,28,28,34,32
     EMBLEMSELECTED=EmblemMusic,28,28,34,32

   Save. The drawer now shows the sample drawer image with a note on it,
   with a soft shadow if you kept the installer's choice.

The icon keeps its own picture all the time: without these tool types,
or if an image is missing, it is shown again. "EmblemIcon Work:Music
TO=AMIGA" makes it a normal icon again.

Note that a default image covers the icon's own picture. To keep the
picture and only add an emblem, leave out IMAGE= and IMAGESELECTED=.
@ENDNODE

@NODE TOOLTYPES "The tool types"
@{b}The tool types@{ub}

  IMAGE=Name[,Method]
  IMAGESELECTED=Name[,Method]

     Show the image Name (ENVARC:sys/emblem/Name.png) instead of the
     icon's own normal / selected picture.

  EMBLEM=Name[,Width,Height,X,Y,Method]
  EMBLEMSELECTED=Name[,Width,Height,X,Y,Method]

     Draw the emblem Name on the normal / selected image.
     Width,Height: the size it is drawn at. 0,0 (or nothing) = its own
       size; when only one of them is 0, that one follows the emblem's
       proportions.
     X,Y: where its top left corner goes on the image (0,0 = top left).
     Method: see @{"Methods" link METHODS}.

  EMBLEM=INTERNAL,...   EMBLEMSELECTED=INTERNAL,...

     The icon's own picture becomes the emblem: for example a program's
     picture, small, on the drawer image. Needs IMAGE= (IMAGESELECTED=
     for EMBLEMSELECTED).

Rules:

 - Names are file names in ENVARC:sys/emblem without ".png" (a name
   with a dot is used as it is). Sub-drawers work: "Work/Busy".
 - Write the keys exactly like this, in upper or lower case, with no
   space before "=". When a key is there twice, the first one counts.
 - A name that isn't found, or an image that can't be read, is ignored:
   without its default image the icon shows its own picture, without
   its emblem the emblem is left out.
 - Without EMBLEMSELECTED the selected image has no emblem.
 - The emblem is cut off at the image's edges.
 - Things you don't want right now can be switched off with brackets:
   (EMBLEM=EmblemMusic,28,28,34,32)
@ENDNODE

@NODE METHODS "Methods (effects)"
@{b}Methods@{ub}

The last field of a tool type chooses an effect for that layer (the
default image, or the emblem):

  SHADOW   a soft shadow under it, down and to the right
  GLOW     a soft light rim around it
  GRAY     in shades of gray
  DARK     a quarter darker
  BRIGHT   a quarter of the way to white
  NONE     as it is

  IMAGE=DrawerNS,GRAY
  EMBLEM=EmblemStar,24,24,20,18,GLOW

Without a method the @{"settings file" link PREFS} decides; the installer
asks which effect emblems get then. Shadows and glows stay inside the
image's rectangle.
@ENDNODE

@NODE PREFS "Settings file"
@{b}The settings file@{ub}

ENVARC:sys/emblem.prefs (and its copy ENV:sys/emblem.prefs, which is
the one that is read) is a text file of KEY=value lines; lines starting
with ; or # are comments.

  METHOD=        method for tool types without one       (NONE)
  IMAGEMETHOD=   ... only for IMAGE= and IMAGESELECTED=  (NONE)
  EMBLEMMETHOD=  ... only for EMBLEM= and EMBLEMSELECTED= (NONE)
                 IMAGEMETHOD and EMBLEMMETHOD win over METHOD.
  SHADOWOFFSET=  x,y of the shadow, -16..16                (5,5)
  SHADOWBLUR=    softness, 0 (hard) .. 8                   (2)
  SHADOWOPACITY= percent                                   (50)
  GLOWSIZE=      how far the glow reaches, 1..8            (2)
  GLOWOPACITY=   percent                                   (80)
  GLOWCOLOR=     RGB in hex, like FFD040                   (FFFFFF)

Without a file, the values in brackets are used.

Changes show within two seconds on the next icon that is loaded (open
the drawer again). Edit ENVARC:sys/emblem.prefs and copy it to ENV:sys:

  Copy ENVARC:sys/emblem.prefs ENV:sys/

(ENV: keeps the copy it read first until the computer restarts.)

A white glow hardly shows on a light window background; try
GLOWCOLOR=FFD040 or a darker colour there.
@ENDNODE

@NODE IMAGES "Images and emblems"
@{b}Images and emblems@{ub}

All images are PNG files in ENVARC:sys/emblem. Any PNG works (true
colour with alpha is best, palette and gray ones are fine too).

 - Default images: up to 256x256 pixels. Workbench icons of AmigaOS 4.1
   are mostly 64x64, so the sample images are 64x64.
 - Emblems: up to 1024x1024; they are scaled to the size in the tool
   type (up to 512x512). The sample emblems are 32x32.
 - The normal and the selected image should have the same size.

Changed or new images are noticed within two seconds and show on the next
icon that is loaded. Emblem keeps up to 64 images (8 MB) in memory.

The sample set is placeholder art, drawn by a script; it is meant to be
replaced. Images:

  DrawerNS, DrawerS         a drawer, closed and open (64x64)
  DocumentNS, DocumentS     a page, normal and selected (64x64)

Emblems (32x32): EmblemMusic, EmblemPictures, EmblemVideo,
EmblemInternet, EmblemMail, EmblemPrefs, EmblemTools, EmblemGames,
EmblemText, EmblemCode, EmblemDownload, EmblemArchive, EmblemStar,
EmblemHeart, EmblemLock, EmblemCheck, EmblemWarning.

Good emblems have a dark rim, so they read on any image and background.
@ENDNODE

@NODE EMBLEMICON "EmblemIcon command"
@{b}EmblemIcon@{ub}

  EmblemIcon FILES/M/A,TO/K,ALL/S,TYPE/K,SET/K,UNSET/K,QUIET/S

Turns icons into the Emblem format and back, shows what they are, and
sets or removes tool types, one icon or whole drawer trees at a time.

  EmblemIcon Work:Music                 format and tool types
  EmblemIcon Work:Music TO=EMBLEM       to the Emblem format
  EmblemIcon Work:Music TO=AMIGA        back to a normal icon
  EmblemIcon Work:#? TO=EMBLEM          every icon in Work:
  EmblemIcon Work: ALL                  list the Emblem icons in Work:
                                        and all drawers below it
  EmblemIcon Work: ALL TO=AMIGA         all of them back to normal icons

Every drawer in Work: on the default drawer image, with its own picture
as the emblem (see @{"INTERNAL" link TOOLTYPES}), in one go:

  EmblemIcon Work: ALL TYPE=DRAWER TO=EMBLEM
     SET="IMAGE=DrawerNS|IMAGESELECTED=DrawerS|EMBLEM=INTERNAL,30,30,33,30"

(one line). And all of them back, without those tool types:

  EmblemIcon Work: ALL TO=AMIGA UNSET="IMAGE|IMAGESELECTED|EMBLEM"

FILES   icons, with or without ".info"; patterns work.
TO      EMBLEM or AMIGA. Without TO the icons are shown (or, with SET or
        UNSET, changed in the format they have).
ALL     also look in the drawers below (links are not followed).
TYPE    only icons of these types: DISK, DRAWER, TOOL, PROJECT, GARBAGE,
        DEVICE, KICK; several separated by commas (TYPE=DRAWER,DISK).
SET     tool types to set, KEY=value, several separated by "|". A tool type
        with that key (upper or lower case) is replaced where it is, other
        ones with the same key are removed; a new key is added at the end.
        Put the value in quotes: SET="IMAGE=DrawerNS".
UNSET   keys of tool types to remove, separated by "|". Done before SET.
QUIET   fewer messages; no tool types when showing.

With a pattern or ALL, only Emblem icons are shown, changed with SET or
UNSET, and converted back with TO=AMIGA; TO=EMBLEM converts the normal
icons (with SET or UNSET it changes the Emblem icons too). A count is
printed at the end.

The conversion keeps everything: the pictures, tool types, position,
default tool, stack and drawer window. Every saved icon is loaded again and
checked. It needs the module to be running (installed, and the computer
restarted).

Return code 10 if an icon failed, 5 when stopped with Ctrl-C, 20 for wrong
arguments.
@ENDNODE

@NODE EXAMPLES "The examples"
@{b}The examples@{ub}

The drawer Examples (next to this guide) has ten Emblem icons. Look at
their tool types (@{i}Icons > Information...@{ui}) to see how each is made:

  Music       an emblem without a method: the settings decide
  Pictures    SHADOW
  Internet    GLOW
  Games       size 0,0 = the emblem's own size; NONE
  Downloads   another emblem
  Private     DARK on the default images
  Favourites  in the middle, and somewhere else when selected
  Old stuff   GRAY on the image and the emblem
  Tools       INTERNAL: the icon's own picture (a wrench) as the emblem
  ReadMe      no IMAGE=: the emblem goes on the icon's own picture

Copy them, or their tool types, as you like.
@ENDNODE

@NODE NOTES "Good to know"
@{b}Good to know@{ub}

 - The icon's own pictures are kept: saving an Emblem icon (snapshot,
   Information window) never stores the composed picture in it. If a
   program gives the icon a new picture (an icon editor), that becomes
   its own picture.
 - Icon editors and other programs see the composed picture. Programs
   that want the icon's own picture can ask for it (see
   @{"For programmers" link PROGRAMMERS}).
 - Copying Emblem icons to a computer without Emblem: the icons can't be
   shown there (you see Workbench's default icons). Convert them back
   with "EmblemIcon ... TO=AMIGA" first.
 - Speed: an Emblem icon loads about as fast as a normal one; one with a
   default image and an emblem takes a little longer, mostly because
   Workbench has more to draw for half-transparent pictures.
 - Low memory: when an image can't be loaded, the icon shows its own
   picture and Emblem tries again a little later.
@ENDNODE

@NODE REMOVE "Removing Emblem"
@{b}Removing Emblem@{ub}

Double-click Install and choose @{i}Remove it@{ui}. It

 - turns the Emblem icons back into normal icons, in the drawers and
   volumes you choose (all drawers below them too), with
   "EmblemIcon ... ALL TO=AMIGA",
 - deletes the documentation drawer with the examples,
 - deletes the sample images (yours too, or none, if you choose so),
 - deletes the settings, EmblemIcon and emblem.iconmodule.

The module stays active until the computer restarts.

Without the module, Emblem icons can't be shown: convert them before you
remove it (Remove does that when you let it).
@ENDNODE

@NODE PROGRAMMERS "For programmers"
@{b}For programmers@{ub}

 - The file format is "Emblem" (an IFF FORM EMBL; Emblem-format.txt
   describes it). icon.library saves an icon in it with
   PutIconTags(name, icon, ICONPUTA_FileFormat, "Emblem", ...).
 - GetIconTags() returns the composed pictures. To get the icon's own
   pictures, pass the tag EMBLEMA_NoCompose (TAG_USER + 0x454D0001) with
   TRUE; other modules ignore it.
 - ICONGETA_FileFormat tells whether an icon is an Emblem icon.
@ENDNODE

@NODE HISTORY "History"
@{b}History@{ub}

